    "result_text": "The build passes and all tests are green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133) computed on creation, keeping the logic well-factored.\n\n**New unit \u2014 the level rule lives in one place:**\n- `util/MembershipLevelCalculator.java` \u2014 a small, single-responsibility helper (mirroring the existing `LocalityResolver`) that computes the level: start at 1, +1 when an email is present, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (`MAX_LEVEL`), with `4` documented as reserved for tenure.\n\n**Wiring, reusing that unit rather than duplicating logic:**\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 the DTO mapping now targets `membershipLevel` via a `computeMembershipLevel` default method that delegates to the calculator (same delegation pattern as `formatLocality`). The old `formatMembershipTier` (GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE) method and mapping are removed.\n- `rest/OwnerAuditLogger.java` \u2014 the create audit line now records `membershipLevel=` (from the same calculator) instead of the tier, per the spec.\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 the response schema's `membershipTier` (string) is replaced by `membershipLevel` (integer, 1\u20133), so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes the new field.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreNumericLevel` (email present + unique name \u2192 level 3) passes, and the audit output confirms `membershipLevel=3` is logged on create.\n\nNote: the pre-existing tier logic also had a `GOLD` case keyed on household size \u2265 3 \u2014 the new spec defines the level purely from email and namesakes (capped at 3), so that household-based branch is intentionally gone, matching the specification exactly.",
